17:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L1f",null,{"guide":{"date":"May 28–30, 1864","place":"Totopotomoy Creek, Bethesda Church, and the approaches to Cold Harbor, Virginia","result":"Early’s attack drove back the exposed U.S. left but failed against Warren’s reformed line; Grant then shifted southeast toward Cold Harbor while both armies brought in reinforcements","facts":[{"label":"Distance from Lee’s line to Richmond","value":"About 9 miles"},{"label":"Beauregard infantry reported available","value":"About 12,000"},{"label":"XVIII Corps troops arriving at White House","value":"About 10,000"},{"label":"Estimated campaign casualties at Totopotomoy","value":"1,890","note":"National Park Service estimate: 731 U.S. and 1,159 Confederate"},{"label":"Shelton House artillery strikes","value":"At least 50"},{"label":"Barlow division casualties near Rural Plains","value":"About 300"},{"label":"Willis brigade casualties","value":"Approaching 60%"},{"label":"Cavalry balance at Matadequin Creek","value":"3 U.S. brigades to 1 Confederate brigade"}],"maps":[{"title":"Troop positions at Totopotomoy Creek and Bethesda Church","caption":"This Army of the Potomac map shows the creek, roads, corps positions, earthworks, and the southeastern route toward Cold Harbor from May 28 to June 2, 1864.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/86690225/","image":"/images/totopotomoy-bethesda-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of troop positions at Totopotomoy Creek and Bethesda Church"},{"title":"The Totopotomoy battlefield today","caption":"Rural Plains stands near the center of the creek line. Bethesda Church and Shady Grove Road marked the open U.S. left where Early attacked Warren on May 30.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.52%2C37.61%2C-77.24%2C37.79&layer=mapnik&marker=37.6777%2C-77.3344"},{"title":"From the Pamunkey crossings toward Richmond","caption":"Grant crossed the Pamunkey north and east of the battlefield, then advanced southwest. Lee’s army occupied shorter interior routes between the creek and Richmond.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-77.63%2C37.50%2C-76.98%2C38.02&layer=mapnik&marker=37.6777%2C-77.3344"}],"moments":[{"time":"1:38","audioTime":98,"title":"Haw’s Shop clarified the armies’ locations","detail":"Confederate prisoners showed Sheridan where Anderson and Ewell were positioned. U.S. prisoners confirmed to Wade Hampton that V and VI Corps had crossed the Pamunkey."},{"time":"2:27","audioTime":147,"title":"Lee reported an incomplete picture","detail":"Lee told Secretary of War James Seddon that Grant appeared to be concentrating near Hanovertown, but he did not yet know the exact U.S. position or strength."},{"time":"3:35","audioTime":215,"title":"Grant had three possible routes","detail":"The U.S. Army could move northwest toward the Virginia Central Railroad, southeast around Lee’s right, or directly against the Confederate line at Totopotomoy Creek."},{"time":"4:12","audioTime":252,"title":"Lee had little room left to maneuver","detail":"Richmond was about nine miles behind the Confederate position. A successful flank movement or breakthrough threatened the capital."},{"time":"5:20","audioTime":320,"title":"Lee sought troops from Bermuda Hundred","detail":"Breckinridge, Pickett, and one Hoke brigade had joined the army, but Lee still faced greater U.S. numbers. He asked for more of Beauregard’s force."},{"time":"6:42","audioTime":402,"title":"Davis rejected Beauregard’s earlier combined plan","detail":"Beauregard had proposed joining Lee for a concentrated blow after Lee withdrew behind the Chickahominy. Davis considered the plan too risky and Beauregard remained reluctant to release troops."},{"time":"9:27","audioTime":567,"title":"Beauregard initially refused the request","detail":"He reported that moving troops away from Bermuda Hundred could expose Richmond and its rail connections to Butler’s Army of the James."},{"time":"11:09","audioTime":669,"title":"Grant removed troops from Butler instead","detail":"A War Department inspection found that about 10,000 men could hold the Bermuda Hundred lines. Grant ordered William F. Smith’s XVIII Corps to join the Army of the Potomac."},{"time":"14:05","audioTime":845,"title":"Smith boarded transports","detail":"On May 28 sufficient vessels arrived before Butler could begin a Petersburg operation. Smith’s command started moving around the Virginia Peninsula to White House Landing."},{"time":"15:29","audioTime":929,"title":"The Army of the Potomac approached the creek cautiously","detail":"Hancock and part of Warren’s corps neared Totopotomoy Creek while Wright remained farther north. Burnside and most of Warren’s corps were still near Haw’s Shop."},{"time":"16:24","audioTime":984,"title":"Lee formed a line south of Totopotomoy Creek","detail":"From west to east the line contained Hill, Breckinridge, Anderson, and the Second Corps under Jubal Early."},{"time":"17:57","audioTime":1077,"title":"Early replaced Ewell","detail":"Richard Ewell left because of illness on May 26. Lee made Early the temporary Second Corps commander and did not later return the corps to Ewell."},{"time":"20:34","audioTime":1234,"title":"Lee identified the U.S. left as an opportunity","detail":"When VI Corps left the Hanover Court House area, Lee concluded that Grant would move southeast. He authorized Early to attack Warren if the chance looked favorable."},{"time":"21:54","audioTime":1314,"title":"Early shifted his corps toward Bethesda Church","detail":"Rodes led the march, followed by Ramseur and Gordon, using new military roads from Shady Grove Church Road toward Old Church Road."},{"time":"22:26","audioTime":1346,"title":"Rodes broke the first U.S. positions","detail":"George Doles’s brigade struck west of Bethesda Church. Rodes drove back a skirmish line, a full brigade, and two brigades of Samuel Crawford’s division."},{"time":"23:38","audioTime":1418,"title":"Warren restored the Fifth Corps line","detail":"U.S. artillery and arriving infantry stopped the Confederate advance. Early lacked enough troops on the field to exploit the initial retreat."},{"time":"24:31","audioTime":1471,"title":"The planned flank attack was not coordinated","detail":"Early wanted Anderson to advance along Old Church Road against Warren’s flank. Communications failed, Anderson did not make the supporting movement, and Early waited."},{"time":"24:54","audioTime":1494,"title":"Willis attacked the U.S. guns","detail":"Edward Willis’s brigade cleared one battery, then came under canister from three more batteries and rifle fire from Pennsylvania infantry."},{"time":"25:47","audioTime":1547,"title":"Willis was mortally wounded","detail":"His brigade reached within about fifty yards of the U.S. line but suffered casualties approaching sixty percent. Hundreds surrendered after the attack stopped."},{"time":"27:25","audioTime":1645,"title":"Hancock demonstrated along the creek","detail":"Grant ordered attacks to reduce pressure on Warren. Hancock’s three divisions advanced near dark, gained little ground, and withdrew after about thirty minutes."},{"time":"28:30","audioTime":1710,"title":"Cavalry fought near Old Cold Harbor","detail":"Matthew Butler’s Confederate brigade moved northeast and met Alfred Torbert’s cavalry near Matadequin Creek, threatening the U.S. route to White House Landing."},{"time":"29:01","audioTime":1741,"title":"Torbert drove Butler back","detail":"Torbert committed all three brigades of his division against Butler’s one and pursued the Confederates nearly to the Cold Harbor crossroads."},{"time":"29:28","audioTime":1768,"title":"Grant chose another southeastern movement","detail":"The Totopotomoy line was too strong for a direct attack. Grant ordered Sheridan to clear the route for Smith and prepared to move around Lee’s right."},{"time":"30:52","audioTime":1852,"title":"Smith added a fifth U.S. infantry corps","detail":"About 10,000 XVIII Corps troops had begun landing at White House. Their arrival increased the pressure on Lee’s three infantry corps."},{"time":"31:21","audioTime":1881,"title":"Lee warned that delay would cause disaster","detail":"Lee bypassed normal procedure and asked Beauregard directly for reinforcements, then told Davis that Smith would reach Grant before Hoke’s division could arrive."},{"time":"32:30","audioTime":1950,"title":"Hoke’s division received orders to join Lee","detail":"Davis ordered Beauregard to send troops. Beauregard had already reconsidered and issued the transfer shortly before the government order arrived."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"blue","branches":[{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"General-in-chief","subordinates":"Directed the southeastern movement and transfer of XVIII Corps"},{"commander":"George G. Meade","command":"Army of the Potomac","subordinates":"Hancock’s II Corps, Warren’s V Corps, Wright’s VI Corps, and Burnside’s IX Corps"},{"commander":"Gouverneur K. Warren","command":"V Corps","subordinates":"Samuel W. Crawford and other divisions defending the U.S. left near Bethesda Church"},{"commander":"Winfield S. Hancock","command":"II Corps","subordinates":"Attacked across Totopotomoy Creek to reduce pressure on Warren"},{"commander":"Philip H. Sheridan","command":"Cavalry Corps","subordinates":"Alfred T. A. Torbert’s division screened the White House supply route"},{"commander":"William F. Smith","command":"XVIII Corps","subordinates":"Transferred from Bermuda Hundred to White House Landing"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"red","branches":[{"commander":"Robert E. Lee","command":"Army of Northern Virginia","subordinates":"Hill’s Third Corps, Anderson’s First Corps, Early’s Second Corps, and Breckinridge’s division"},{"commander":"Jubal A. Early","command":"Second Corps","subordinates":"Rodes, Ramseur, and Gordon divisions"},{"commander":"Robert E. Rodes","command":"Division, Second Corps","subordinates":"George Doles and other brigades that opened the Bethesda Church attack"},{"commander":"Stephen D. Ramseur","command":"Division, Second Corps","subordinates":"Edward Willis’s brigade made the unsuccessful attack on Warren’s reformed line"},{"commander":"Richard H. Anderson","command":"First Corps","subordinates":"Did not make the supporting movement Early expected along Old Church Road"},{"commander":"P. G. T. Beauregard","command":"Department of North Carolina and Southern Virginia","subordinates":"Released Robert F. Hoke’s division from Bermuda Hundred"}]}],"people":[{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Moved the Army of the Potomac southeast and brought XVIII Corps from Bermuda Hundred"},{"name":"Gouverneur K. Warren","role":"Reformed V Corps after the first Confederate advance near Bethesda Church","side":"United States"},{"name":"Winfield S. Hancock","role":"Sent II Corps against the creek line to reduce pressure on Warren","side":"United States"},{"name":"William F. Smith","role":"Commanded the XVIII Corps reinforcements landing at White House","side":"United States","image":"/images/baldy-smith-cold-harbor.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018670739/"},{"name":"Robert E. Lee","role":"Held the Totopotomoy line, authorized Early’s attack, and requested reinforcements","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/robert-e-lee-1864.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666540/"},{"name":"Jubal A. Early","role":"Took command of Second Corps and directed the May 30 attack on the U.S. left","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"P. G. T. Beauregard","role":"Initially retained his Bermuda Hundred force, then sent Hoke’s division to Lee","side":"Confederacy"},{"name":"Edward Willis","role":"Brigade commander in Ramseur’s division whose attack reached the U.S. artillery line","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[{"name":"Edward Willis","side":"Confederacy","role":"Colonel commanding a brigade in Ramseur’s division","outcome":"Mortally wounded while leading the attack near Bethesda Church on May 30"}],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/totopotomoy-bethesda-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical troop-position map of Totopotomoy Creek and Bethesda Church","caption":"The map shows the corps lines, roads, and earthworks that shaped the fighting and the movement toward Cold Harbor.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/86690225/"},{"image":"/images/totopotomoy-waud-drawing.jpg","alt":"Alfred Waud drawing at Totopotomoy Creek","caption":"The drawing records buildings and battlefield activity along the Totopotomoy Creek line.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660390/"},{"image":"/images/overland-campaign-1864-map.jpg","alt":"Historical map of the Overland Campaign route","caption":"Grant’s move from the North Anna to the Pamunkey and Totopotomoy continued the repeated effort to pass Lee’s right.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/99448882/"},{"image":"/images/cold-harbor-battlefield.jpg","alt":"Historical photograph of the Cold Harbor battlefield","caption":"The cavalry action on May 30 opened the next stage of the campaign near Cold Harbor.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2018666598/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast, episode 494","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.org/2025/06/08/civil-war-podcast-episode-494/"},{"label":"National Park Service: Totopotomoy Creek battle detail","url":"https://www.nps.gov/civilwar/search-battles-detail.htm?battleCode=va057"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Totopotomoy Creek","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/tcbattlebull.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Rural Plains battlefield","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/rural-plains.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: U.S. and Confederate positions, May 30","url":"https://www.nps.gov/rich/learn/historyculture/tctroopmap.htm"},{"label":"U.S. Army Center of Military History: The Overland Campaign","url":"https://history.army.mil/portals/143/Images/Publications/catalog/75-12.pdf"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Totopotomoy and Bethesda Church map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/86690225/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Alfred Waud at Totopotomoy Creek","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/2004660390/"}]}}]}]
